      "writtenOn": "2023-09-07T18:35:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I should have given more context from the beginning.\n\nThe common driver already supports full duplex, except if CONFIG_SPI_HALFDUPLEX is defined in board.h.  This means that the spi_devices[] array \"ordinary\" boards will not mention full duplex support, as it is either supported by all or not supported by any of the ports.  Code in usb_spi.c will set a bit in the feature_bitmap of struct usb_spi_response_configuration_v2, sent via USB, to advertise whether full duplex is supported, depending on the compile time define.\n\nNow, I previously added support for \"custom\" board-level SPI drivers, and added a flag to spi_devices[].usb_flags to allow the board to declare for each SPI port, whether a custom driver should be used.  This means that the common logic in usb_spi.c may a mix of SPI ports using the common driver, and ports using special board-specific drivers.  For all the ports that use the common driver, usb_spi.c already \"knows\" whether full duplex is supported, based on the compile time define.  For each of the board-specific ports, usb_spi.c cannot know, so that is why I am proposing a new flag in spi_devices[], which has meaning only for SPI ports using board-specific drivers, that allows the board to tell usb_spi.c whether each of the special ports support full duplex.\n\nI want to avoid adding explicit USB_SPI_FEATURE_FULL_DUPLEX_SUPPORTED to each of the ports using common driver, on all boards that do not have CONFIG_SPI_HALFDUPLEX.  As that would leave open the possibility of future inconsistencies, e.g. if the define CONFIG_SPI_HALFDUPLEX is later modified in board.h, without appropriate updates to spi_devices[].  That is why I propose a new flag, which should only have meaning for custom-driver ports.",
